;; The IR interpreter — and therefore the DIFFERENTIAL ORACLE.

;;
;; This exists because golden strings and the verifier both stay green through a merge deleted from
;; a diamond, a Return dropped from Stop, and a type check discharged wrongly. Only running the
;; program notices. Every optimisation is gated on the observable result being identical before and
;; after the pass, over the whole corpus.
;;
;; Arguments are bound to values INSIDE their declared types. Feeding an argument something its
;; declaration forbids does not test the optimisation; it tests what happens when you lie to the
;; compiler.
;;
;; THE HEAP IS IMMUTABLE AND VERSIONED. A demand-driven walker cannot read a mutable heap: it
;; visits nodes in an order the program does not, so "the current value of this field" is not a
;; well-defined question. Each Store produces a new heap version and a Load names the version it
;; reads, which is the same discipline memory SSA already imposes on the graph.
;;
;; IT REFUSES TO GUESS. An operation it cannot perform faithfully reports a named status rather
;; than producing a plausible number — a wrapped integer, a guessed shape — because an oracle that
;; quietly disagrees with the language it defines is worse than no oracle.
;;
;; OBJECT IDENTITY ACROSS TWO RUNS is the reachable heap, not the allocation index. An index is a
;; run artefact: removing an allocation nothing reads shifts every later one, so the oracle would
;; report a failure for a transformation that changed nothing observable. Instead the outcome is
;; rendered with objects numbered by DISCOVERY ORDER in a deterministic walk and shapes named by
;; their field names — which is isomorphism of the two reachable heaps, which is what "the same
;; outcome" means. It also makes sharing observable: `{a:o, b:o}` renders differently from
;; `{a:o, b:o2}`.
;;
;; SCAFFOLD: the statuses are final; bodies hard-error. Filled in at S1 and grown with the IR.
(module aot.eval)
(import "aot.util.panic" :use *)
(import "aot.node.node" :use *)

(const EV-OK 0)

(const EV-OVERFLOW 1)  ; integer overflow: refuses to wrap

(const EV-SHAPE 2)  ; a load through a pointer whose shape lacks the field

(const EV-MEM 3)  ; a memory state that does not carry the class the node names

(const EV-CAST 4)  ; a failing Cast — a COMPILER bug, not a program one

(const EV-THROW 5)  ; the program threw

(const EV-UNSUPPORTED 6)  ; the interpreter cannot perform this faithfully

(defn ev-reset! [] (-> i64)
  (do (unimplemented "ev-reset!" "S1 — src/eval.coil") 0))

(defn ev-run [(root (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (do (unimplemented "ev-run" "S1 — src/eval.coil") EV-UNSUPPORTED))

;; Render the outcome — status, result, and the heap reachable from it — as text that outlives
;; `ev-reset!`. Carrying a heap across runs is the one thing that must not happen.
(defn ev-render-outcome [] (-> (slice u8))
  (do (unimplemented "ev-render-outcome" "S1 — src/eval.coil") ""))
